CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is clear, but a real observable difference occurs only when filtering is active and runtime metadata contains overloaded objects whose ordinary and overloaded names interact differently with the configured patterns.

Object filtering now supports evaluating multiple names for one database object. During navigator tree loading, overloaded objects are filtered using both their ordinary object name and overloaded name; inclusion succeeds when either name matches, while exclusion rejects the object when either name matches.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When tree items are filtered, an overloaded database object is now evaluated under both its normal name and its overloaded name. It remains visible only if the resulting multi-name filter evaluation accepts it; non-overloaded objects continue to be evaluated by their normal name alone.
  • The filter contract now accepts multiple candidate names. An inclusion pattern may match any supplied name, but an exclusion pattern matching any supplied name rejects the candidate.

Investigate
  • Find every caller of DBSObjectFilter.matches and determine whether any callers rely on its former single-String binary signature or will begin supplying multiple aliases.
  • Trace which navigator-loading flows produce DBPOverloadedObject children and what overloaded names represent for each database implementation.
  • Check the intended precedence when inclusion matches one name but exclusion matches another, and whether overloaded names are guaranteed to be non-null.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not identify all callers of the public matches method or establish whether any caller other than the shown navigator-loading path supplies multiple names.
  • The observable effect depends on a non-null filter being active and the loaded child being a DBPOverloadedObject; the supplied diff does not establish how often those conditions occur in real installations.
  • The contract of DBPOverloadedObject.getOverloadedName, including whether it can return null and how it differs from the ordinary object name, is not shown.
  • It is not established whether DBSObjectFilter is consumed by separately compiled plugins or other binary clients for which the method descriptor change would be compatibility-sensitive.
  • Which database metadata providers expose overloaded objects, and what names those providers return at runtime, cannot be determined from the changed files.

